---

### Merge (SQL-like joins)

```python
customers = pd.DataFrame({
    'customer_id': [1, 2, 3],
    'name': ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Charlie']
})

orders = pd.DataFrame({
    'order_id': [101, 102, 103, 104],
    'customer_id': [1, 2, 1, 4],
    'amount': [100, 200, 150, 300]
})

# Inner join (matching rows only)
merged = pd.merge(customers, orders, on='customer_id', how='inner')

# Left join (all from left, matching from right)
merged = pd.merge(customers, orders, on='customer_id', how='left')

# Right join
merged = pd.merge(customers, orders, on='customer_id', how='right')

# Outer join (all rows from both)
merged = pd.merge(customers, orders, on='customer_id', how='outer')

# Join on different column names
merged = pd.merge(customers, orders, 
                  left_on='customer_id', right_on='customer_id')
```

